WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.980
Metrics help teams understand whether a sovereign AI system is healthy, slow, overloaded, unstable, or approaching a resource limit.

2
00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:12.760
Logs explain events, but metrics show patterns over time.

3
00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:23.180
They help operators decide whether to tune the workflow, resize resources, reduce load, split workloads, restart services, or change user expectations.

4
00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:34.180
Core service metrics include request latency, throughput, queue depth, error rate, timeout rate, retry count, model load time, failed generation, and degraded responses.

5
00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:42.600
For local or private model servers, model load behavior is often important because startup and reload time can dominate user experience.

6
00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:46.000
AI-specific metrics should be handled carefully.

7
00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:57.700
Context size, input and output length, token use at a high level, truncation events, retrieval counts, and response failures can all explain quality and performance issues.

8
00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:02.600
These metrics do not require storing full prompts or outputs by default.

9
00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:07.040
Hardware metrics are often central in sovereign AI.

10
00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:21.300
CPU load, RAM pressure, accelerator utilization, GPU or VRAM pressure where available, disk space, disk throughput, network conditions, thermal behavior, and power constraints can all affect reliability.

11
00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:26.440
A model may look unreliable when the real cause is resource exhaustion.

12
00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:28.720
Metrics should support decisions.

13
00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:36.260
A dashboard with dozens of numbers is less useful than a small set of signals connected to operating questions.

14
00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:38.600
Can the system handle current load?

15
00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:41.340
Is quality being affected by resource pressure?

16
00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:42.560
Is a queue growing?

17
00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:47.060
Are failures concentrated in one model, source, or worker?
